---
// src/pages/story.astro — The Story
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import PullQuote from '../components/PullQuote.astro';
import Reveal from '../components/Reveal.astro';
import CTASection from '../components/CTASection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Story"
  description="Ember Ridge is not a theory developed in a boardroom. It is the enlargement of a pattern one family has lived for nearly two decades — restoration, refuge, intercession, and beauty from ashes."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="The Story Behind the Vision"
    title="Beauty From Ashes"
    lede="Ember Ridge is not a theory developed in a boardroom. It is the enlargement of a pattern our family has lived for nearly two decades."
  />

  <Section tone="ivory" labelledby="restoration-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <Reveal>
        <SectionHeading
          id="restoration-heading"
          eyebrow="The Foundation"
          title="Restoration, lived before it was taught"
        />
      </Reveal>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-16">
        <div class="lg:col-span-5">
          <Reveal>
            <figure>
              <div class="overflow-hidden border border-stone-200 shadow-[var(--shadow-lift-lg)]">
                <img
                  src="/images/shannon-and-jason.jpg"
                  alt="Shannon and Jason Suttles"
                  width="620"
                  height="620"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                  class="aspect-square w-full object-cover"
                />
              </div>
              <figcaption class="mt-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-stone-600">
                Shannon and Jason Suttles
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          </Reveal>
        </div>

        <div class="lg:col-span-7">
        <Prose>
          <p>
            The foundation of this vision is restoration. Shannon Suttles knows what
            it means to be considered beyond repair. Her early life was marked by
            severe abuse, rejection, addiction, instability, homelessness, and deep
            emotional wounds. There were seasons when professionals and spiritual
            leaders alike had little hope for her future.
          </p>
          <p>
            Yet Jesus Christ did not abandon what others had written off. He met her,
            delivered her, taught her to hear His voice, rebuilt her identity,
            restored her capacity to love, and transformed the places of greatest pain
            into a ministry of hope for others.
          </p>
          <p>
            That testimony formed the way Shannon and Jason understand the Gospel.
            Restoration has never been an abstract message to them. It is the lived
            reality of a God who enters broken places, tells the truth in love, and
            patiently rebuilds a life until purpose stands where devastation once ruled.
          </p>
        </Prose>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="linen" labelledby="refuge-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <Reveal>
        <SectionHeading
          id="refuge-heading"
          eyebrow="A Home Before a Ministry"
          title="A table, a bed, and people willing to remain"
        />
      </Reveal>
      <div class="mt-12">
        <Prose>
          <p>
            Long before there was a plan for Ember Ridge, their home became a place of
            refuge — for people facing homelessness, addiction, mental illness, domestic
            violence, family crisis, and the loss of direction.
          </p>
          <p>
            They housed women and children escaping danger, walked beside parents whose
            children were missing, and welcomed families who needed more than a brief
            appointment.
          </p>
          <p>
            They needed a table, a bed, consistent prayer, correction without
            rejection, and people willing to remain present long enough for healing to
            take root.
          </p>
          <p>
            This same pattern expanded into churches, schools, businesses, and
            leadership environments. Shannon spent roughly a decade training intercessors
            and teaching believers to hear the voice of God, discern spiritual roots,
            minister healing, and stand in the gap for families and nations.
          </p>
          <p>
            Jason provided spiritual covering, operational strength, and a steady example
            of fatherhood and covenant.
          </p>
        </Prose>

        <div class="callout my-12 grid items-center gap-8 sm:grid-cols-12 sm:gap-10">
          <div class="sm:col-span-4 lg:col-span-3">
            <Reveal>
              <div class="flex items-center justify-center rounded-sm bg-white p-5 ring-1 ring-stone-200">
                <img
                  src="/images/jllc-logo.jpg"
                  alt="Jesus Loves the Little Children — School Chaplains"
                  width="640"
                  height="640"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                  class="h-auto w-full max-w-[9rem] sm:max-w-none"
                />
              </div>
            </Reveal>
          </div>

          <div class="sm:col-span-8 lg:col-span-9">
            <h3 class="font-sans text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-gold-700">
              Jesus Loves the Little Children
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-stone-700">
              The family’s school chaplaincy was born from prayer rather than
              institutional ambition. What began as intercession developed into a
              ministry presence within schools, where children, families, educators, and
              administrators encountered hope, healing, order, and practical support. The
              work demonstrated a principle central to Ember Ridge: when prayer,
              spiritual discernment, relational presence, and professional
              responsibility operate together, environments can change.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="cream" labelledby="proximity-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <Reveal>
        <SectionHeading
          id="proximity-heading"
          eyebrow="Confirmed Within the Family"
          title="Some healing occurs only through proximity"
        />
      </Reveal>
      <div class="mt-12">
        <Prose>
          <p>
            The vision has also been confirmed within the family itself. Anne Whaley,
            J.D., experienced restoration not simply through counsel but through
            returning to healthy household life after a difficult season. Living under
            the same roof brought hidden wounds to the surface within an atmosphere of
            love, truth, prayer, boundaries, and everyday discipleship.
          </p>
          <p>
            Her testimony gave the family language for what they had already witnessed
            in many others. People often need more than a message; they need to
            observe covenant, share meals, experience consistency, receive correction
            safely, and discover what healthy family life feels like from the inside.
          </p>
        </Prose>

        <PullQuote>
          Ember Ridge enlarges that household without turning it into an institution.
        </PullQuote>

        <Prose>
          <p>
            It gives structure, space, and stewardship to a ministry pattern that has
            already borne fruit. The work will remain personal enough for people to be
            known, intentional enough for placements to be prayerfully discerned, and
            professionally governed enough to protect both those who come and those
            who serve.
          </p>
          <p class="pt-2">
            <a href="/leadership" class="btn-quiet">Meet the family and leadership →</a>
          </p>
        </Prose>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <CTASection
    eyebrow="The Pattern"
    title="The same work, given room"
    body="What has happened around one family table for nearly two decades is what Ember Ridge is being built to hold. The vision explains how."
    primary={{ label: 'Read the Vision', href: '/vision' }}
    secondary={{ label: 'Meet the leadership', href: '/leadership' }}
  />
</BaseLayout>
